Mid-March, an early spring morning on a hillside footpath outside the city. The night before, her father got stuck in a video game because he could not push a boulder out of the way; she told him the trick was simply to walk up to it, keep holding on and push forward, and that tiny question later grew into a whole guidebook. Now, on a real path, she has come upon a waist-high round mossy boulder half blocking the way, and she has decided to try it herself. Her mood: playful determination, a little amused at herself, completely absorbed in the effort. Her body: both palms pressed flat against the rough stone, one shoulder leaning into it, the back foot braced in the damp soil, knees bent, head tilted down toward the rock, a few loose strands of hair across her cheek; the boulder has just begun to budge, leaving a small fresh scrape in the soil at its base. She forgets the camera. Camera: medium-wide shot from slightly to the side and a little further up the path; she and the boulder are on the left third of the frame; in the soft-focus right foreground a branch of yellow winter jasmine in bloom; beyond the boulder the path continues up the hill. Early spring in Beijing: dry, clear, cool air, bare trees with the first tiny green buds, pale blue sky, low golden morning sun from the side, long soft shadows. Hair in a loose single braid over one shoulder; a light misty-blue cotton jacket over a cream thin knit sweater, charcoal straight-leg trousers, oatmeal canvas sneakers, a small canvas shoulder bag pushed back behind her.
